What are Agricultural Chelates?
Agricultural chelates are specialized compounds where essential metal ions like iron, zinc, and manganese are bound to organic molecules called ligands. This chemical structure makes micronutrients more stable and bioavailable to plants, particularly in challenging soil conditions where nutrients might otherwise become inaccessible. The most widely used chelating agents in agriculture include EDTA, DTPA, and EDDHA, each offering distinct advantages depending on soil pH and crop requirements.
